::PC_U04_L06_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
Solve sin(theta)=1/2 for 0 degrees <= theta < 360 degrees.
{
~A. 30 degrees only#This misses the Quadrant II solution.
=B. 30 degrees and 150 degrees#Correct. Sine is positive in Quadrants I and II with reference angle 30 degrees.
~C. 210 degrees and 330 degrees#These are where sine is negative.
~D. 90 degrees and 270 degrees#These angles have sine values 1 and -1.
}
::PC_U04_L06_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
A right triangle has opposite side 6 and adjacent side 8. Which ratio finds theta?
{
~A. sin(theta)=8/6#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, not adjacent over opposite.
~B. cos(theta)=6/8#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse, not opposite over adjacent.
~C. tan(theta)=8/6#This reverses the tangent ratio.
=D. tan(theta)=6/8#Correct. Tangent is opposite over adjacent, so tan(theta)=6/8.
}
::PC_U04_L06_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
Why should a real-world trigonometric solution be checked?
{
=A. Context may restrict meaningful lengths or angles#Correct. Context can limit possible angles, lengths, or time intervals.
~B. All trig equations have no solutions#Many trigonometric equations have solutions.
~C. Every angle is always valid#Some mathematical solutions may not make sense in context.
~D. Checking changes the equation#Checking does not change the equation; it verifies the solution.
}
::PC_U04_L06_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
If tan(theta)=1 for 0 degrees <= theta < 180 degrees, what is theta?
{
~A. 30 degrees#tan(30 degrees) is not 1.
~B. 60 degrees#tan(60 degrees) is not 1.
=C. 45 degrees#Correct. In this interval, tan(theta)=1 at 45 degrees.
~D. 135 degrees only#tan(135 degrees) is -1, not 1.
}
::PC_U04_L06_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
A ladder of length 10 feet makes a 60 degree angle with the ground. Which expression gives the height?
{
~A. 10cos(60 degrees)#Cosine gives the adjacent horizontal distance.
=B. 10sin(60 degrees)#Correct. Height is opposite the angle, so height = hypotenuse times sine.
~C. sin(60 degrees)/10#This divides instead of multiplying by the hypotenuse.
~D. 10/tan(60 degrees)#This uses tangent without the adjacent side.
}